Hi all,

Attached is a tar directory containing all the necessary files for 
evaluating elastostatic single and double layer potentials in either 
free space or a half-space.  

We've included a triangulated sphere as a test geometry but 
the solver will take any input file in *.tri format

---------------------------------------------------------------------
*.tri (or Cart3D format) asks that the first line contain

NVERTS  NTRIANGLES

followed by a list of vertex coordinates,
followed by a list of vertex indices that correspond to each triangle.

see sphere2880.a.tri for an example or 

http://people.nas.nasa.gov/~aftosmis/cart3d/cart3Dhome.html
for the (mostly not relevant) home page.
---------------------------------------------------------------------

By a single layer potential, we mean the displacement/strain due to
a piecewise constant traction vector over the surface.
By a double layer potential, we mean the displacement/strain due to
a piecewise constant jump in displacement vector over the surface.

The file elfmm3dtria_e2_dr.f contains a sample driver for free space.
It reads in the sphere geometry and creates artificial traction and 
displacement vectors on each triangle. 
The displacement and strain are evaluated on all triangles as well
as a collection of (off-surface) targets.
The file elhfmm3dtria_self_iter_dr.f contains a sample driver for 
the half space Green's function with the field being evaluated
on the triangle centroids themselves.
The file elhfmm3dtria_targ_iter_dr.f contains a sample driver for 
the half space Green's function with the field being evaluated
at a collection  of (off-surface) targets. 
These are all in the examples directory.

Both half-space codes (with **iter** in the name) have the option
of storing the local interactions in an initial call, so that
subsequent calls are noticeable faster.

There are some matlab interfaces (in the matlab directory) that
we use for testing/plotting etc. Our current plan is not to 
support them for other users.

Most files in the src directory (which holds all the fortran source)
are cleaned to the point of using implicit none, allocation of 
large workspaces, and correct dimensioning of arrays in the calling
sequences. Some are still (implicit real *8 (a-h,o-z)) and d3tstrcr.f
and d3tstrcr_mindlin.f still mix up integer and real workspaces.
These will be fixed soon.

One point of explanation - the reason for a separate tree building 
routine (d3tstrcr_mindlin.f) is to enforce that the center of the
FMM box lies exactly on the z=0 plane. This cleanly separates
boxes which contain image source points in the upper half-space
from original source and  target points in the lower half-space.

One important point to be aware of. The subroutine tria3adam 
(in tria3adamar.f) uses adaptive integration to evaluate displacements
and strains due to nearby image triangles. It currently allows for 
200 levels of adaptive subdivision. In some pathological cases, the code
might bog down or hang there. (e.g. an extremely thin triangle right 
at the interface z=0). That can be disabled by setting nrec to something
smaller (say, nrec = 3). We haven't encountered such a problem yet, but
it could happen with a bad triangulation.


Best,

Leslie and Zydrunas
